<rules version="5">
    <!-- Search rules v5, specification: rules-spec.md. Layers: this base, then rules_<language>.xml and
         rules_<language>_<COUNTRY>.xml of the locale of the data (map and name tag, never the user interface).

         A stored name keeps the words of OSM: no rule rewrites it.
           <locales>  only here: map name prefix -> rules locale, statistics group of common_words_groups.tsv,
                      transliteration of names.
           <index>    applied by the OBF writer:
                        <class0>/<class1>/<class2>  words that are always a key / service words / frequent words of
                                                    the locale, over the classes of common_words_groups.tsv;
                        <unglue glue="." minPart="2"/>  splits glued words into an alternative name;
                        <rule object="street" from="regexp" to="form"/>  adds an alternative name: a phrase, a glued
                                                    or a tokenizer-lost form; keys="always" makes its new words keys.
                      A word of an alternative name is a key by its own class, whatever happened to the words it
                      replaces.
           <query>    applied by the search with the locale of every OBF it reads:
                        <rule from="pl" to="Place" object="street"/>, <rule from="st"><to object="street">Street</to>
                        <to>Saint</to></rule>: forms of a query word, each with a generated reverse form;
                        object="building" without to: a part of a house number;
                        to="": an ignorable word of every owner (an optional query token, never a penalty);
                        <skipPenalty object="street">de la</skipPenalty>: words of names that do not penalize an
                        object of the owners when the query does not have them.

         Keys: a query rule - its word; an index rule - object and from; <unglue> - glue; a class word and a
         <skipPenalty> word (with object) - the word. A lower layer replaces an upper rule of its key whole,
         enabled="false" removes it, two rules with one key in one file are an error. -->
